* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - writes a version 2 SCRAM record.  The Argon2
*           verifier is gone: SCRAM needs material a client can prove
*           knowledge of without sending the password, and a one-way verifier
*           cannot supply that.  docs/SCRAM_AUTH.md
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - the credential store is $cred on disk.  5.12 (a).
* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - written for the account credential model
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - the write falls back to the ELEVATED HELPER, because
*           $cred grants sdusers nothing and a session that reached SDSYS by
*           LOGTO from an unelevated start is refused by Windows however
*           administrator SD believes it is.  The record is then READ BACK and
*           both keys compared before success is reported.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 68
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* Derives and stores SCRAM material for an account.  What is written:
*
*   SaltedPassword = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(password, salt, iterations, 32)
*   StoredKey      = SHA256(HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Client Key"))
*   ServerKey      = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Server Key")
*
* THE PASSWORD IS NOT STORED, and neither is ClientKey.  StoredKey is the
* SHA-256 of ClientKey, so holding it does not let the holder authenticate as
* the client - forming a proof needs ClientKey itself, which is the preimage.
* It does let the holder impersonate the SERVER, which is why the $CRED ACL
* matters as much as it ever did.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 7 step 6.
*
* This is the only place in the server that runs PBKDF2.  Login does not: it
* holds StoredKey directly and needs two HMACs and a hash, so the cost of a
* high iteration count falls on setting a password, not on connecting.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine CRED_SET(account, password, ok)
$catalog !CRED_SET

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

   ok = @false
   set.status 0

   account = upcase(trim(account))
   if account = '' or password = '' then
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end

   salt = ''
   call !SD_GET_SALT(salt)
   if salt = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   iter = SCRAM$ITERATIONS

   salted = SDEXT(password : @FM : salt : @FM : iter : @FM : SCRAM$KEY.LEN, @TRUE, SD_PBKDF2)
   if salted = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   client.key = SDEXT(salted : @FM : 'Client Key', @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)
   stored.key = SDEXT(client.key, @TRUE, SD_SHA256)
   server.key = SDEXT(salted : @FM : 'Server Key', @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)

*  Drop the intermediates as soon as they are spent.  Neither may be stored,
*  and ClientKey in particular is the one value that would let its holder
*  authenticate as this account.
   salted = ''
   client.key = ''

   if stored.key = '' or server.key = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   cred.rec = ''
   cred.rec<CRED$VERSION> = SCRAM$CRED.VERSION
   cred.rec<CRED$MECH> = 'SCRAM-SHA-256'
   cred.rec<CRED$SALT> = salt
   cred.rec<CRED$ITER> = iter
   cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> = stored.key
   cred.rec<CRED$SERVERKEY> = server.key

* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - THE WRITE FALLS BACK TO THE ELEVATED HELPER, AND IS
*   THEN READ BACK BEFORE IT IS BELIEVED.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 68.
*
*   WHY THE DIRECT WRITE IS NOT ENOUGH.  $cred is locked to SYSTEM and
*   Administrators and grants sdusers NOTHING (gplbld/secure-cred.ps1).  A
*   session that reached SDSYS with LOGTO from an unelevated start has
*   K$ADMINISTRATOR set - CPROC:2769 sets it, and it IS USR_ADMIN - so SD is
*   correctly convinced it is administrator; but the PROCESS TOKEN never
*   changed, so this write is refused by Windows.  MEASURED 30 Aug 2026 by
*   gplbld/verify-sdsyswrite.ps1: "Unable to set password for SDSWA2, status
*   3035", and err.h:145 is ER_PERM, "Permissions error (os.errno)".
*
*   THE OPEN SUCCEEDS AND THE WRITE DOES NOT, which is why the failure arrives
*   late - the caller has already collected both passwords by then.
*
*   ps_script IS THE ROUTE THE REST OF THE TREE ALREADY USES for work that needs
*   privilege: PS_SCRIPT:166 hands the script to the elevated helper when
*   K$ADMINISTRATOR is set, and falls back to os.execute when it is not, so an
*   ordinary caller behaves exactly as before.  SET_PASSWD's Windows half has
*   gone this way since 27 Aug.
*
*   IT IS SAFE TO PUT CREDENTIAL MATERIAL THROUGH IT.  PS_SCRIPT writes into
*   SDSYS\pstmp, which secure-psdir.ps1 gives CREATOR OWNER (OI)(IO)(F) with
*   sdusers holding directory rights only - so a script there is readable by its
*   creator, SYSTEM and Administrators and by nobody else.  That ACL was added
*   16 Aug 2026 for exactly this reason: !set_passwd's script already carries a
*   new Windows password in clear.
*
*   ***CRLF, AND IT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.*** A directory-file record
*   is a file and a field mark is a line ending; on disk SD writes CRLF - od -c
*   on sdsys/os.users/don reads "y e s \r \n y e s \r \n".  Writing LF-only from
*   PowerShell would put a record here that SD reads back with the wrong field
*   count, silently, in the credential store.  [char]13 and [char]10 are used
*   rather than an escape so nothing depends on how the script survives being
*   written to a file and Invoke-Expression'd.
*
*   ASCII IS SAFE BECAUSE EVERY VALUE IS BASE64.  gplsrc/sd_scram.c:26 - "EVERY
*   BINARY VALUE HERE IS BASE64, in and out" - so the salt and both keys are
*   text, and none of them can contain the quote this builds with.
   write.ok = @true
   write cred.rec to cred.f, account on error
      write.ok = @false
   end

* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - CLOSE BEFORE THE ELEVATED WRITE, AND RE-OPEN TO READ
*   BACK.  The first version of this fix kept cred.f open across the outside
*   write and read back through the same file variable, and it FAILED on
*   -Run sdswa3 while the identical change in MODIFYA passed - which is what
*   named the difference.  MODIFYA closes first and its own comment says why:
*   letting another process write the file while SD holds it open is a view SD
*   believes in and Windows knows nothing about.  The rule was applied in one
*   file and not the other on the same day.
   close cred.f

   if not(write.ok) then
      cred.path = kernel(K$WINPATH, @sdsys : @ds : '$cred' : @ds : account)
      if cred.path = '' then
*        NOT ER$PERM.  The path could not be built, which is not a permissions
*        failure, and the first version reported all four of these the same way
*        - so the status said 3035 and could not say WHICH stage failed.  That
*        is the fault this file's own header criticises in its caller.
         set.status ER$ARGS
         return
      end

      q = char(39)
      ps =       '$p = ' : q : cred.path : q
      ps := @fm : '$l = @(' : q : cred.rec<CRED$VERSION>   : q : ','
      ps :=                   q : cred.rec<CRED$MECH>      : q : ','
      ps :=                   q : cred.rec<CRED$SALT>      : q : ','
      ps :=                   q : cred.rec<CRED$ITER>      : q : ','
      ps :=                   q : cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> : q : ','
      ps :=                   q : cred.rec<CRED$SERVERKEY> : q : ')'
      ps := @fm : '$nl = [char]13 + [char]10'
      ps := @fm : 'try {'
      ps := @fm : '  [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, ($l -join $nl) + $nl,'
      ps := @fm : '     [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII)'
      ps := @fm : '} catch { exit 1 }'
      ps := @fm : 'exit 0'

      if ps_script(ps) # 0 then
*        ER$PERM here and ONLY here: the elevated write itself was refused.
         set.status ER$PERM
         return
      end
   end

*  ***THE READ-BACK IS THE POINT, NOT A COURTESY.*** Either path above can
*  report success and leave a record this side cannot use: the direct write can
*  be refused, and the elevated one goes through a PowerShell script whose exit
*  code says the FILE was written and nothing about whether SD can parse it.
*  An unusable $cred record is an account that authenticates against nothing, and
*  it would be found at the next login rather than here.  So the record is read
*  back through SD's own reader and BOTH keys are compared - the same fields
*  !CRED_VERIFY will use.
*  RE-OPENED, because the file was closed above before anything outside SD was
*  allowed to touch it.  A fresh open is also the only way to be sure this reads
*  the bytes on disk rather than anything SD was holding.
   check.rec = ''
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to chk.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end
   read check.rec from chk.f, account else check.rec = ''
   close chk.f

*  ER$WRITE.ERROR, NOT ER$PERM.  Reaching here means a write reported success
*  and the record does not read back as what was meant to be stored - which is
*  a different fault from being refused, and the caller's message should not
*  say "permissions" about it.
   if check.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> # stored.key then
      set.status ER$WRITE.ERROR
      return
   end

   if check.rec<CRED$SERVERKEY> # server.key then
      set.status ER$WRITE.ERROR
      return
   end

*  NO close HERE.  cred.f was closed unconditionally before the elevated write,
*  and chk.f was closed after the read-back, so both handles are already shut by
*  every path that reaches this line.
   ok = @true

   return
end

* END-CODE
